
{{/*
  The card grid, shared by the catalog and the index — so restyling a product card
  changes both, rather than one of them and not the other in a way nobody notices.
  Override *this* name to change how a card looks anywhere it appears.

  It takes the whole page rather than a list of products, because the eager/lazy
  rule below needs the index within the grid and the price needs .Currency. The
  index passes four products, so every card there is eager, which is what a page
  of four above the fold wants anyway.

  A grid that reflows on its own: styles.css asks for as many columns as fit above a
  minimum width, so there are no breakpoints here and none in the CSS either.
*/}}
{{define "product_grid"}}
<ul class="products">
  {{range $i, $p := .Products}}
  <li>
    {{/*
      The whole card is the link, so the target is the card rather than a few words
      of title.
    */}}
    <a class="product-card" href="/products/{{$p.Slug}}" hx-boost="true">
      {{/*
        The fixed-ratio frame is what makes a catalog of differently-sized
        photographs line up, and what stops a product with no picture yet from being
        a shorter card than its neighbours. The placeholder carries no alt text: the
        title directly below already says what the thing is, so announcing "no image"
        to a screen reader would be noise.

        The first row is deliberately *not* lazy. Lazy-loading an image that is
        already on screen defers exactly the one that decides how quickly the page
        looks finished. Four is a guess at a row, and a stated one: the grid asks for
        as many columns as fit, so the real count is a CSS decision the server cannot
        know — four over-fetches slightly on a phone and under-fetches on a wide
        monitor, and the alternative is a breakpoint the CSS deliberately does not
        have.

        No width/height either, on purpose: .frame reserves the box with aspect-ratio
        in both layouts, so there is no shift left to fix, and these are object-fit
        cover images whose real dimensions the store never records.
      */}}
      <span class="frame">
        {{if $p.HasImage}}
          <img src="{{image $p.ImageKey}}" alt="{{$p.Title}}" decoding="async"
               {{if ge $i 4}}loading="lazy"{{end}}>
        {{else}}
          <img src="{{asset "placeholder.svg"}}" alt="" decoding="async"
               {{if ge $i 4}}loading="lazy"{{end}}>
        {{end}}
      </span>
      <span class="title">{{$p.Title}}</span>
      <span class="price">
        {{if $p.OnePrice}}
          {{$.Currency}} {{money $p.MinPriceCents}}
        {{else}}
          {{$.Currency}} {{money $p.MinPriceCents}} – {{money $p.MaxPriceCents}}
        {{end}}
      </span>
      {{/*
        Sold out is shown rather than the product being hidden: somebody looking for
        it should find out it exists and is gone, not conclude the shop never had it.
      */}}
      {{if not $p.InStock}}<span class="badge sold-out">Sold out</span>{{end}}
    </a>
  </li>
  {{end}}
</ul>
{{end}}
